Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama, naturalistic faces, winter light, and historically grounded fabric.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON COUNTING-HOUSE COURT - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Brown fog swallows the narrow court. Above a warehouse door, faded gilt letters read SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

(SOUND: distant church bell, carriage wheels, boots stamping in frost)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

EBENEZER SCROOGE, a thin elderly man with a pointed nose, red-rimmed eyes, wiry white hair, blue lips, and a black frock coat, guards a tiny coal fire. BOB CRATCHIT copies letters beside one fading coal, wrapped in a long white comforter.

(SOUND: pen scratching, wind whistling through window seams)

FRED, ruddy-faced and bright-eyed in a dark green greatcoat, enters through the fog.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time but a time for paying bills without money, and finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

FRED
I have always thought of it as a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.

BOB involuntarily claps once, then pokes his fire until its last spark dies.

(SOUND: poker scraping grate, coal snapping out)

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED leaves, calling a warm greeting to BOB.

(SOUND: office door shuts, muffled carol outside)

Two portly charity collectors, MR. HARKER and MR. WELLS, remove their hats at SCROOGE'S desk.

MR. HARKER
At this festive season, we seek provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. WELLS
Plenty of prisons, sir.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. HARKER
They are. Many would rather die than go there.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR. WELLS lowers his pen. The two men depart into the fog.

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

SCROOGE walks alone past holly-lit shop windows and a brazier where ragged children warm their hands. A small CAROL SINGER leans toward his keyhole.

(SOUND: gas flames hissing, distant carol singing)

CAROL SINGER
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

SCROOGE raises his ruler. The CAROL SINGER runs.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

A gloomy room of Dutch biblical tiles, bare furniture, and a low fire. SCROOGE, now in a worn black dressing-gown and nightcap, eats gruel beside the hearth.

(SOUND: spoon against bowl, clock ticking, wind rattling window)

The brass knocker in the door darkens, stretches, and becomes JACOB MARLEY'S pale face: white hair, ghostly spectacles, and a rigid jaw.

SCROOGE stares. The face snaps back into a knocker.

(SOUND: sudden metallic knock, door slamming, echoes through the house)

SCROOGE locks the door twice. He looks under the bed, then sits before the fire.

A disused bell begins to tremble overhead.

(SOUND: bell ringing softly, then every house bell clanging)

A chain scrapes up from the cellar. The bedroom door remains shut as MARLEY'S GHOST passes through it: transparent, in his old waistcoat and pigtail, bound in a chain of cash-boxes, keys, ledgers, and padlocks.

(SOUND: iron chain dragging across floorboards, fire flaring)

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese.

MARLEY throws back the bandage around his jaw. His jaw falls upon his breast.

(SOUND: MARLEY'S terrible cry, chain crashing against the hearth)

SCROOGE falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

MARLEY lifts the chain. Its ledgers and locks sway around him.

MARLEY
Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

MARLEY points through the window at the black sky.

MARLEY
You have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee!

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

MARLEY moves backward. The window lifts open by itself.

(SOUND: window sash groaning, wind flooding the room)

Beyond the glass, chained phantoms drift over London, reaching toward unseen homes.

MARLEY
Look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us.

MARLEY floats into the night. SCROOGE shuts the window, trembling.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS NIGHT - NIGHT

SCROOGE wakes in darkness beneath his curtains. A church clock strikes twelve, then pauses.

(SOUND: deep clock chimes, SCROOGE'S anxious breathing)

At one stroke, white light fills the room. The curtains pull aside.

(SOUND: fabric rings sliding, a single clear bell tone)

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands beside the bed: child-sized yet ancient, with white hair, a smooth youthful face, bare feet, a white tunic, holly branch, and a bright flame rising from its crown.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brought you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this.

The Spirit touches SCROOGE'S heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - WINTER DAY

Snow lies beneath a clear blue sky. Older SCROOGE, still in his black dressing-gown, stands beside the luminous Spirit and watches boys race home in carts.

(SOUND: boys shouting, pony hooves crunching snow)

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been.

INT. BOARDING-SCHOOL CLASSROOM - WINTER DAY

A long, bare room with cracked windows and plain desks. YOUNG EBENEZER, a small pale boy with dark hair and patched brown school clothes, reads alone by a feeble fire.

(SOUND: mice behind paneling, wind through broken panes, a weak fire clicking)

SCROOGE watches his younger self. Tears shine on his lined cheeks.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

The book's illustrations seem to stir: ALI BABA rides past the window; ROBINSON CRUSOE'S parrot flashes green and yellow.

SCROOGE
There's the Parrot! Poor Robin Crusoe, where have you been?

The schoolroom door flies open. FAN, a delicate little girl with dark curls and a red wool cape, runs to YOUNG EBENEZER.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! To bring you home, home, home!

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be.

SCROOGE reaches toward them but cannot touch them.

SCROOGE
She had a large heart.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

A warm warehouse transformed for dancing. Oil lamps glow over swept boards. FEZZIWIG, round, silver-haired, rosy-cheeked, and broad in a burgundy waistcoat, claps his hands as YOUNG EBENEZER and DICK WILKINS raise the shutters.

(SOUND: fiddle tuning, shutters banging, delighted laughter)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve!

Young employees whirl in a country dance. MRS. FEZZIWIG dances with FEZZIWIG as the fiddler plays faster.

(SOUND: vigorous fiddle dance, shoes thundering on wood)

SCROOGE smiles through tears as his young self dances.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

SCROOGE looks at FEZZIWIG giving each departing worker a handshake.

SCROOGE
He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome.

SCROOGE lowers his eyes.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

A modest parlour warmed by a winter fire. BELLE, a young woman with soft brown hair, a plain mourning dress, and steady dark eyes, faces YOUNG SCROOGE, now leaner and sharply dressed.

(SOUND: fire crackling, distant children laughing beyond a wall)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you, I have no just cause to grieve.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing the world condemns with such severity as the pursuit of wealth.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. The master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

BELLE turns away, pressing a handkerchief to her mouth.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

(SOUND: fire collapses softly, a clock ticking)

The vision shifts. BELLE, older but still graceful, sits by a fire amid laughing children. Her HUSBAND enters carrying parcels.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I passed his office window. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE grips the Spirit's sleeve.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

SCROOGE seizes the great extinguisher cap and forces it over the Spirit's flame.

(SOUND: rushing white light, SCROOGE'S ragged cry)

Light pours beneath the cap. SCROOGE collapses into darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS NIGHT - NIGHT

Ruddy light spills from the adjoining room. SCROOGE opens the door in his dressing-gown.

(SOUND: roaring fire, distant church bell striking one)

His room has become a green bower of holly, ivy, fruit, steaming punch, geese, pies, and pudding. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits atop the feast: a towering, broad-chested giant with long dark curls, a green fur-trimmed robe, holly wreath, bare feet, and a glowing torch.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! And know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I am afraid I have not walked forth with your family.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred brothers have I.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT rises and holds out his robe.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY - DAY

The Spirit and SCROOGE stand invisible amid snowy streets. Shopkeepers carry baskets of oranges, chestnuts, and geese while families hurry toward warm homes.

(SOUND: snow shovels scraping, market calls, church bells)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sprinkles light from his torch over two quarrelling dinner-carriers. They smile and move on.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - CHRISTMAS DAY - DAY

A small four-room home, threadbare but clean. MRS. CRATCHIT, brisk and proud in a twice-turned gown with bright ribbons, lays the table with MARTHA and PETER. Two YOUNG CRATCHITS dance around a bubbling pot.

(SOUND: potatoes boiling, children laughing, kettle lid rattling)

BOB enters in his worn coat and long white comforter, carrying TINY TIM on his shoulder. TINY TIM is a slight boy with pale cheeks, brown curls, a little crutch, and iron braces beneath neat worn clothes.

BOB
And how did little Tim behave?

TINY TIM smiles from BOB'S shoulder.

BOB
As good as gold, and better. He hoped the people saw him in church, because he was a cripple.

TINY TIM is set on his stool by the fire. The goose arrives, and the family gathers around the table.

(SOUND: carving knife through crisp skin, a joyful murmur around the table)

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

THE CRATCHITS
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

SCROOGE watches TINY TIM hold BOB'S hand.

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE bows his head.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

The words strike SCROOGE. MRS. CRATCHIT raises a small glass.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his.

BOB
My dear. Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy New Year!

The family drinks, then draws close around the hearth.

(SOUND: chestnuts crackling in the fire, TINY TIM'S soft song)

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT - NIGHT

FRED'S candlelit home gleams with polished wood and holly. FRED'S WIFE, a pretty young woman with dark curled hair and a blue silk dress, laughs beside FRED and their guests.

(SOUND: piano melody, glasses clinking, warm group laughter)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred.

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it.

FRED'S WIFE
I have no patience with him.

FRED
I have. I am sorry for him. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

SCROOGE watches unseen, his face softening.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not.

The guests play a guessing game. FRED struggles not to laugh.

(SOUND: stamping feet, a burst of laughter, cards slapping table)

FRED
It is a live animal, rather disagreeable, that walks about London.

FRED'S WIFE
I have found it out! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

Everyone laughs. SCROOGE almost laughs with them.

FRED fills a glass of mulled wine.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is!

SCROOGE silently raises an imaginary glass.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Fred.

EXT. LONDON CLOCK TOWER - CHRISTMAS NIGHT - NIGHT

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT has grown old and grey. Beneath his robe crouch IGNORANCE, a starved boy in rags, and WANT, a starved girl in rags, both with hollow eyes.

(SOUND: freezing wind, clock chimes three quarters past eleven)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The final stroke of twelve falls. The Spirit vanishes.

(SOUND: final clock stroke booming, wind abruptly cut off)

A tall black hooded PHANTOM glides through the snow, one skeletal hand pointing onward.

EXT. LONDON EXCHANGE - DAY

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME stands beside SCROOGE in a crowded market of merchants. Its black garment conceals everything but its outstretched hand.

(SOUND: coins clinking, papers rustling, distant carriage traffic)

MR. BLAKE, a fat merchant with a monstrous chin, speaks with MR. PIPER, red-faced and snuff-stained, and MR. GRANT, narrow and well dressed.

MR. BLAKE
I only know he's dead.

MR. PIPER
When did he die?

MR. BLAKE
Last night, I believe.

MR. GRANT
What has he done with his money?

MR. BLAKE
Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me.

The men laugh.

(SOUND: merchants laughing, coins dropped into a pocket)

MR. PIPER
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral.

SCROOGE looks to the silent Phantom.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen.

The hand points onward.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT

A low, filthy shop of rusty keys, scales, rags, and bones. OLD JOE, grey-haired and crooked, smokes by a charcoal stove. MRS. DILBER, a hard-faced charwoman in a black shawl, drops a bundle on the floor.

(SOUND: stove coals hissing, rusty shop hinges screeching)

A LAUNDRESS and an UNDERTAKER'S MAN bring smaller bundles. OLD JOE examines their stolen goods.

MRS. DILBER
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

LAUNDRESS
No man more so.

MRS. DILBER opens her bundle. Bed-curtains spill across the floor.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. DILBER
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You took them down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime?

OLD JOE counts coins into each thief's hand.

(SOUND: coins clattering on bare boards, the three thieves laughing)

MRS. DILBER
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

SCROOGE recoils.

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A bare bed stands in a dark, neglected room. A sheet covers a body. A cat scratches at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearth.

(SOUND: cat clawing wood, rats gnawing, cold wind moaning in the chimney)

The Phantom points at the covered face.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me.

The Phantom's robe sweeps across the room.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - FUTURE NIGHT

The CRATCHIT HOME is quieter, poorer, and dimmer. MRS. CRATCHIT sews by the fire with MARTHA, PETER, and the YOUNG CRATCHITS. TINY TIM'S little crutch rests alone in the chimney corner.

(SOUND: needle through cloth, mantel clock ticking, a fire settling)

PETER reads from a book.

PETER
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

BOB enters, older in posture but wearing his familiar long white comforter and worn coat. He tries to smile.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

BOB
And so have I. Often.

BOB goes upstairs. SCROOGE follows with the Phantom.

INT. TINY TIM'S BEDROOM - FUTURE NIGHT

Tiny Tim's small bed is neatly made. Christmas holly hangs above it. BOB sits beside the empty chair and kisses the pillow.

(SOUND: BOB'S unsteady breath, floorboards creaking below)

BOB
My little, little child!

BOB buries his face in his hands. SCROOGE stands frozen in the doorway.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A weed-choked graveyard enclosed by blackened houses. The Phantom stands beside a neglected stone, pointing down.

(SOUND: winter wind through iron gates, a distant funeral bell)

SCROOGE approaches on shaking knees. The name on the stone becomes visible: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(SOUND: low thunder rolling, SCROOGE'S breath catching)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to SCROOGE and back again.

SCROOGE falls before the stone.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

SCROOGE clutches the Phantom's robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. Tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Phantom's hand trembles. Its hood collapses into darkness.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDCHAMBER - CHRISTMAS DAY - MORNING

SCROOGE clings to his own bedpost. His black dressing-gown, wiry white hair, and pointed face are unchanged, but sunlight floods the room.

(SOUND: church bells pealing, bright street voices below)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future!

He runs to the window and throws it open.

(SOUND: window flying up, bells ringing louder, crisp morning air)

A BOY in Sunday clothes stands below.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it!

SCROOGE laughs, then spots the Poulterer's shop.

SCROOGE
Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it! Come back with the man, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The BOY races off.

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY - DAY

SCROOGE, now in his best black frock coat, silk hat, and polished boots, walks through bright snow with a great turkey being loaded into a cab behind him.

(SOUND: cab horse snorting, sleigh bells, SCROOGE'S delighted laugh)

MR. HARKER approaches in his winter cloak. SCROOGE stops him and takes both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, a merry Christmas to you!

MR. HARKER
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE leans close and whispers his donation. MR. HARKER's eyes widen.

(SOUND: passing children singing a carol, cab wheels crunching snow)

MR. HARKER
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

MR. HARKER
I don't know what to say.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS DAY - NIGHT

FRED'S table is laid for dinner. FRED and his WIFE look up as the door opens. SCROOGE stands in the doorway, hat in hand.

(SOUND: dinner plates clinking, fire crackling, sudden silence)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED crosses the room and seizes his uncle's hand.

(SOUND: chairs scraping back, joyful laughter rising)

FRED
Let him in? Why, Uncle Scrooge!

SCROOGE smiles as FRED'S WIFE embraces him.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DECEMBER 26 - MORNING

The familiar office is warmer. SCROOGE sits at his desk in his black frock coat; a full coal scuttle waits beside BOB'S fire. BOB enters late, still lean and pale, in his worn coat and long white comforter.

(SOUND: clock striking nine, BOB'S hurried footsteps, pen nib tapping)

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir.

BOB approaches cautiously. SCROOGE leaps up and grips his waistcoat.

(SOUND: chair legs scraping, BOB'S startled gasp)

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB stares.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

SCROOGE fills BOB'S coal scuttle with his own hands.

(SOUND: coal tumbling into iron, fire catching brightly)

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

BOB'S face breaks into astonished joy.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

EXT. LONDON STREET - WINTER DAY

SCROOGE walks beside BOB and TINY TIM. SCROOGE wears his best black coat and silk hat; BOB wears his familiar white comforter; TINY TIM carries his little crutch but walks between them, smiling.

(SOUND: children playing in snow, church bells ringing warmly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.
